## The rights basis differs by state — read the relevant state's own README, not just this file

This is not one blanket claim. Each state's source documents come from a different kind of
publisher with a different rights basis, checked and stated honestly per state rather than
assumed to be uniform:

| State | Document type | Basis for redistribution | Detail |
|---|---|---|---|
| Washington | State agency (DSHS) court-monitor reports | Public record, agency-hosted | `washington/README.md` |
| Oregon | State agency / court monitor / AG legal filings | Public record + fair use (court filings) | `oregon/README.md` |
| Colorado | Federal court Special Master reports (via RECAP) | Fair use (court filings) — not a public-domain claim | `colorado/README.md` |
| Colorado | State legislative (JBC) budget memos | Fair use only — the weakest footing of the five sources, said plainly | `colorado/README.md` |
| Texas | State agency (HHSC) reports | HHSC's own conditional license (non-commercial, unaltered, attributed) — NOT public domain | `texas/README.md` |
| California | State agency (DSH) budget documents | Public domain, per CA's own stated policy and case law | `california/README.md` |

**In short: California is public domain; Texas is conditionally licensed by its own publisher's
stated terms; Washington and the Oregon/Colorado agency-hosted documents are public agency
records; the Oregon and Colorado court filings rest on fair use, backed by the same basis
CourtListener/RECAP/Justia already rely on to redistribute filings at scale; and Colorado's JBC
budget memos rest on fair use alone, without a public-domain or agency-license safety net behind
it.** None of this is a formal legal opinion — it's a documented, good-faith, per-document
determination, exactly like the pre-publication safety review below.

